{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgui3jzWY8f6aDyLx4Ska","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've nominated a champion (v8), and all deliverables are committed: `agent/CHAMPION`, `journal.md`, `report.md` and `suggestions.md`. The harness interface check passed for both the 24h and 1h formats. I stopped at 7.3 h with $74 of the $100 spent, because the last eight experiments each moved CRPS by under 0.1% or made it worse.\n\n**The model** runs on CPU only, about 0.1 s per 24h prompt and 0.03 s per 1h prompt. It has no trained weights, just two small files fitted on the snapshot. Its main parts:\n- a weekly volatility pattern for each asset, by minute of the week in New York time;\n- a volatility level that mixes fast, medium and slow running averages, weighted by how far ahead each step is;\n- volatility multipliers after scheduled releases, read from the event calendar;\n- simulated paths with fat tails, prices rounded to the exchange's price step for SOL and XRP, and an extra uncertainty factor for weekends.\n\n**Official backtester** (`agent/predict.py`, 1000 paths, the validator's own prompt times, the 10 days before 2026-11-08):\n\n| Configuration | Starter CRPS | Champion v8 CRPS | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CRYPTO_24H | 4606.76 | 4432.82 | −3.78% |\n| COM_EQU_24H | 3304.04 | 3035.10 | −8.14% |\n| CRYPTO_1H | 988.40 | 925.40 | −6.37% |\n\nThat window is in-sample: the champion's profiles and event multipliers were fitted on data up to 2026-11-08. The honest estimate comes from a held-out window (September to early October) that I never used for any decision; the model was fitted only on earlier data. There, v7 (v8 minus the weekend factor) matched the best single miner in the field: −0.01%, +0.00% and −0.16% CRPS on the three configurations. It beat the starter by 3.2%, 12.7% and 4.4%.\n\n**What made the difference:**\n- **Time-of-week pattern and level forecast:** the New-York-time pattern plus the mixed short/long running averages gave the largest gains, −6.7% on the commodities/equities set.\n- **Robust volatility measure:** using average absolute moves instead of squared moves stopped occasional busy weekends from inflating forecasts for the typical quiet one.\n- **Calendar events:** these cut CRPS by 6–10% on 1h prompts that contain a CPI release and by 4–10% on prompts that contain an earnings release.\n- **Spreading the 1000 random draws evenly** gave a small, free gain of about 0.07% in every asset on every window. It removes a known upward bias when CRPS is computed from a finite set of paths.\n\n**Measured failures, logged in the journal:**\n- A fast within-path volatility process made every asset worse.\n- High-low ranges and trade counts added nothing beyond recent price moves.\n- Two fixes for the Friday-afternoon-into-weekend problem helped in one period and hurt in another.\n\n**Benchmark note:** I compared against the best single field miner, not the per-prompt 10th percentile. No individual miner reaches that percentile, because it mixes different miners on each prompt.\n\n**Known weak spot:** the worst prompts are still equity prompts starting Friday afternoon, where a busy Friday session is followed by a quiet weekend; the model forecasts 3–4 times too much movement. NYSE holidays aren't modelled, but none fall in the evaluation window.\n\nEverything is in `agent/`; open `report.md` first."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1475,"cache_read_input_tokens":450378,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1475,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0c085d9a-8c1d-4329-8588-fab9897b1131","uuid":"b47273f4-26b8-421a-8888-339f82485eb4","timestamp":"2026-11-10T18:19:16.834Z"}
